WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.691
The CPU runs main. The call to device

00:00:03.691 --> 00:00:06.484
copies the starting zero from CPU memory

00:00:06.484 --> 00:00:09.675
into a separate array in GPU memory. The

00:00:09.675 --> 00:00:12.468
two boxes show where those arrays live.

00:00:13.968 --> 00:00:16.203
CUDA loads the compiled kernel when

00:00:16.203 --> 00:00:18.810
needed. The CPU launch requests one block

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:22.162
with one thread. A block is a group of

00:00:22.162 --> 00:00:24.769
threads. CUDA assigns the block to a

00:00:24.769 --> 00:00:27.377
streaming multiprocessor, or S M, on the

00:00:27.377 --> 00:00:27.749
GPU.

00:00:29.249 --> 00:00:32.142
The highlighted statement runs on the GPU

00:00:32.142 --> 00:00:34.622
thread. Before it writes, both arrays

00:00:34.622 --> 00:00:37.102
contain zero. Which array will contain

00:00:37.102 --> 00:00:39.169
forty-two after the statement runs?

00:00:42.669 --> 00:00:45.266
The thread writes forty-two at index zero

00:00:45.266 --> 00:00:47.862
in the device array. The original CPU

00:00:47.862 --> 00:00:50.459
array still holds zero. The new value

00:00:50.459 --> 00:00:53.426
stays in GPU memory until the host copies

00:00:53.426 --> 00:00:54.168
it back.

00:00:55.668 --> 00:00:58.569
Copy to host waits for the kernel to

00:00:58.569 --> 00:01:01.108
finish and returns a CPU array called

00:01:01.108 --> 00:01:03.646
result. Main prints that array. In the

00:01:03.646 --> 00:01:05.460
lesson, change forty-two to seventy-three,

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.361
run the code, and check the value printed

00:01:08.361 --> 00:01:09.449
in the Console.
